// @test: a `my<String>` handle yields its payload on copy-out, as a field, and as a method receiver (#427)
// @description: the concatenation path treated a handle operand's pointer as a string, so
//   `println("c=" + u)` printed the prefix followed by heap bytes, and reading a `my<String>`
//   struct field (`p.meta`) printed bytes too -- the field access already unwraps the handle for
//   the member, but the resulting pointer still reached the serializer as a value. Both operands now
//   load through the handle first (`loadHandleStringPayload`) before the type dispatch. Item 2 of
//   #427 closes the two remaining halves with the same rule: a handle implicitly yields its payload
//   String in a String context, so `t<String> = u` copies the payload out, `f<String> = p.meta`
//   copies a handle FIELD's payload out, and `u.len()` dispatches to the payload's String method.
//   Two handles must not be added directly (`my<String> + my<String>` is refused by semantic
//   analysis), so each handle is paired with a plain literal.
// @category: ownership, string
// @expect-output: c=concat-ok m=field-ok hen=9 copy=concat-ok fld=field-ok
// @expect-return: 0

share(all)
struct P { meta<my<String>> }

main()<Int> -> {
    u<my<String>> = my("concat-ok")
    p<P> = P { meta: my("field-ok") }
    t<String> = u
    f<String> = p.meta
    println("c=" + u + " m=" + p.meta + " hen=" + u.len().to_string() + " copy=" + t + " fld=" + f)
    return 0
}
